---
/**
 * ACADEMIC STRUCTURE · SUBJECT COMBINATIONS
 *
 * ⚠ THE BANNER IS THE SHARED PageHero AND ITS TREATMENT IS UNTOUCHED — same
 * component, same photograph, same crop, same crumbs, same height, same title
 * and standfirst as before and as every other Academics page. All of the
 * redesign is below it.
 *
 * The page was rebuilt off the generic AcStatement → AcCollage → AcClose system
 * into a composed one: five sections, five grounds, five different ideas. The
 * whole composition lives in
 * components/academics/structure/SubjectCombinationsPage.astro.
 *
 * ⚠ THIS IS THE PAGE THAT OWNS THE UNCERTAINTY, and the redesign did not soften
 * it — it made it the structure. data/academics.ts sets out three confidence
 * levels, and every panel, stamp and rung on the page is driven by them:
 *
 *   1 · DEFINITIONAL — PCM and PCB. The abbreviation IS the subject list, and
 *       English is compulsory in CBSE Classes XI–XII.
 *   2 · STANDARD REFERENCE — the Commerce core. Printed, and labelled as the
 *       CBSE structure rather than as a list the school has published.
 *   3 · NOT PUBLISHED — the Humanities core, and every elective combination.
 *
 * ⚠ NO ELECTIVE LIST APPEARS ON THIS PAGE. That decision is kept from the
 * previous build, deliberately and unchanged.
 *
 * ⚠ THE HUMANITIES PANEL CARRIES NO SUBJECT LIST AT ALL — not a greyed one, not
 * a caveated one. A plausible Humanities combination is the single easiest thing
 * on this page to get wrong, and a family may choose a school on it.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../../../components/ui/PageHero.astro';
import SubjectCombinationsPage from '../../../components/academics/structure/SubjectCombinationsPage.astro';

import banner from '../../../assets/computer lab/DSC_1204 copy.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout
>
  <PageHero
    src={banner}
  />

  {/* ⚠ THE PANEL IS REQUIRED. PageHero is sticky and this panel covers it as the
      page scrolls; without it the banner hangs under the footer.

      ⚠ AND THE PADDING RESET IS AN INLINE STYLE, DELIBERATELY. `.u-page-panel`
      in base.css carries `padding-bottom: var(--sb-section-y)` — 120px of empty
      cream between the closing section and the footer. Every stylesheet form of
      this override (scoped, then global) measured correctly in a clean browser
      and still showed the gap on the client's screen, because a stylesheet can
      be served from a different build than the HTML that needs it. An inline
      style travels IN the HTML, so the two cannot skew, and it outranks every
      selector without needing !important. This is the one place on the page
      where that trade is worth making. */}
  <div class="u-page-panel" style="padding-block: 0;">
    <SubjectCombinationsPage />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
